Chapter: ArithOperandOrder

 Regression guard for the arithmetic result type.

 infer-arithmetic used to return the LEFT operand's type verbatim, so the
 verdict depended on the order the author wrote the operands in. Over a
 0..255 byte, `b.val + 1` kept 0..255 and compiled, while `1 + b.val` --
 the same sum -- took the literal's type and was rejected, because an
 integer literal is typed int-ty-default: the full i64 range.

 Both spellings are here, and both must compile and agree. A regression
 that restored the left-operand rule would not make this test print the
 wrong number -- it would stop bump-left compiling at all, which the
 harness reports as FAIL_COMPILE. Only having the two side by side shows
 it: a test carrying either one alone passes under the bug.

 What this does NOT claim: that the sum cannot leave the bound. It can --
 255 + 1 is 256, and the store's runtime check traps on it, which is the
 contract the bound declares. That is why the inputs here are in range.
 The rejection of a genuinely untyped source (plain Integer into a
 bounded field) is codex/test/errors/narrowing-unproven's subject and is
 unaffected: intent the author never declared is still not carried.

Section: Definitions

  Byte = record {
   val : Integer between 0 and 255
  }

  bump-right : Byte -> Integer
  bump-right (b) = (Byte { val = b.val + 1 }).val

  bump-left : Byte -> Integer
  bump-left (b) = (Byte { val = 1 + b.val }).val

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni (integer-to-text (bump-right (Byte { val = 42 })))
   print-line-uni (integer-to-text (bump-left (Byte { val = 42 })))
  end
